Two deques made by this module compare equal when they hold equal items in the
same order, however their ends are split, and that is also the key rule for
Counter and defaultdict. `length(deque)` answers how many items the deque holds;
it reads the stored sizes rather than walking the items, and a hand-written
record that claims to be a deque is validated before its sizes are trusted.
This is what a list work queue does not have: `shift` reads the first item but
does not remove it, so the only removal from the front is `skip(xs, 1)`, which
copies the rest of the list on every call. A deque has no JSON form, so
`json.stringify` refuses one rather than writing `null`, which would say it is
`nothing`; the REPL's `.json` refuses it for the same reason.

A named tuple is an ordinary record whose fields are named at run time:
`collections.namedtuple(names, values)` refuses a name that is not text, a
repeated name, and a length mismatch. `collections.field(record, name)` answers
the field, and a missing field is a `RuntimeError` naming it. That is the
difference from reading a record's property, which answers `nothing` for a field
that is not there: a named record says when the name is wrong instead of letting
`nothing` travel on as a value.

### sys

```redblue
set options to [{name: "count", type: "number"}]
set parsed to sys.parse(options)
for each argument in sys.argv()
    say argument
end
```

`sys.argv()` is the list of arguments after the program path, and it is empty
for a program run from the REPL or from a test, because only `rb run` collects
them. `sys.parse(declaration)` reads those arguments against a list of flag
records and answers a record with one field per flag, plus `arguments`, `help`
and `usage`; `sys.parse(declaration, arguments)` reads an explicit list instead.
A flag has a `name`, a `type` (`text`, `number`, `yes/no` or `path`), and
optionally `takes_value`, `default`, `required` and `help`. A declaration
error, an unknown flag, a missing required flag and a value of the wrong type
are catchable `RuntimeError`s naming the flag, and `--help`/`-h` answers the
generated usage without requiring the rest. The `Cli` module in `modules/`
wraps this: its `run` function prints the usage and skips the body
when help is asked for, and calls `body(parsed)` otherwise; its `run_with`
function takes the argument list explicitly.

### time

```redblue
set stamp to time.now()                  // a record: seconds, nanoseconds
time.sleep(0.5)                          // pause for half a second
say time.format(1704067200, "%Y-%m-%d")  // "2024-01-01"
say time.format(time.date(2024, 2, 9))   // 2024-02-09 00:00:00
say time.parse("2024-01-01T12:00:00+02:00") // 2024-01-01 10:00:00
say time.unix("2024-01-01 00:00:00")     // 1704067200
say time.from_unix(1704067200)           // 2024-01-01 00:00:00
```

`time.now()` is a record of `seconds` and `nanoseconds` since
1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, and `time.sleep` answers `nothing` once the pause is over.
`time.format` takes a date value or a number of seconds since the same instant
and a format string; with no format string it writes `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S`.
`time.parse` reads an ISO-8601 timestamp as a date, applying any zone offset so
the value is the UTC reading of the instant. `time.parse(text, layout)` reads
text in a caller-chosen layout instead, the shape another system emits.
`time.unix` is the other direction:
it takes a timestamp text or a date value and answers the seconds since the same
instant, read as UTC wherever it runs. A date that holds a fractional second
answers a fractional number of seconds rather than dropping it, so
